--- MESSAGE ---
drake: the case body element, specified and not yet built

Three actors' worth of discussion settled a construct whose need had been visible since the forest
scenario landed: `ash/RootInterface` dispatches on which subtype of its message arrived, and it does so
as a single-line Scala `match` carried through as host-opaque text. That parses, and parsing it proves
nothing, because the text projects to no target but Scala.

WHAT WAS DECIDED, AND WHAT CHANGED THE DECIDING

The case is a twelfth BodyElement kind, not a reuse of `Pattern`. GitHub #58 argued for reusing
`Pattern` verbatim and for doing `signal` first; Dev overruled both. A rule pattern has NO scrutinee and
is unordered; a case has exactly one and is ordered. They share no type and no spelling. Only `message`
admits one, and it is optional there — an actor whose message body is plain statements does not
dispatch, which is 13 of the corpus's 14 actors. `start` and `signal` are untouched, and the hardcoded
one-case `signal match` in Generator.actorBehavior stays hardcoded.

The binder is OPTIONAL, and the answer came from counting rather than from principle. With one use site
there was nothing to calibrate against, so the reversible choice (required) looked right. Then Dev
pointed at the projection domains: 44 sibling-subtype dispatch sites already sit in the hand-written
engine, and the canonical one — Drake.keyword — binds in 1 of 9 branches. Requiring a binder would put
a dead name in the other eight at the site draco most wants to project. `TypeElement.name` already
defaults to "", so an unbound branch is absence, not a flag.

The default branch is a branch with no type, and CASE ALONE tells the four forms apart — the same rule
that already bounds a reference (lower-case is a package word; the first upper-case token ends the run):

    case a AlarmSignal [ ... ]   binder + type        case e [ ... ]   default, bound
    case AlarmSignal [ ... ]     type, unbound        case [ ... ]     default, unbound

Whether a default is NEEDED falls out of the statements-or-result split that a dyn-with-body already
has: effect branches never need one (a non-matching scrutinee is a no-op, the target writes its own
catch-all); result branches always do unless exhaustive over the scrutinee's subtypes. That rule was
found by testing the first draft against Drake.keyword, whose ninth branch is `case e => sys.error(…)`
— a catch-all that produces a value, which the draft had declared unauthorable.

A case brackets itself, and the reason is the one the bracket rule exists for: `mon session.fire` after
the last branch belongs to the enclosing block, and whitespace cannot say so. BRACKETS' "two openers"
became three.

WHAT IT COSTS, RECORDED WHERE IT WILL BE LOOKED FOR

`case` is not a free token. The reserved set in Drake.scala is flat and global — a value span stops at
the first reserved word anywhere — and two pinned definitions carry `case` inside host-opaque text:
TypeName.equals and DefinitionPath.source. Neither can stay as it is once the token is reserved, and
neither is a loss: DefinitionPath's `Seq()`/`Seq(url)` is structural arity and converts to the `if`
operator drake already declares; TypeName.equals converts to a case-branch with a default, which takes
its value out of the host-opaque tail entirely. DIVERGENCES carries this so the next session does not
rediscover it by breaking DrakeGenTest.

That conversion puts a case in a DYN body, which the spec as written admits only in `message`. Widening
it is the one open decision; the reasons to are that it is where the 44 engine sites live and it is the
use Dev named for the drake/generator domains.

SPEC ONLY

No Case kind exists, `case` is not reserved, and neither Drake.emit nor Drake.parse carries one. The
increments from here: free the token (two files, held by DrakeGenTest/DrakeParseTest); the Case trio,
BodyElement.modules, the reserved word, emit and Generator; then parse, and RootInterface converts.

